2. Delivery Phases

Phase 0 — Scope, Governance, and Domain Decisions

Objective

Convert the specification into an implementable domain model with explicit terminology, state transitions, evidence rules, and unresolved design decisions.

Deliverables

• Domain glossary.
• System boundary definition.
• Modality registry.
• Portal registry.
• Evidence and confidence policy.
• Recognition-state policy.
• Objective-versus-subjective data policy.
• Historical and future-data policy.
• Privacy and consent policy for subjective exposure.
• Decision log for unresolved questions.

Key decisions

The following must be resolved before production implementation:

1. Whether subjective exposure is:
  • user-entered only;
  • optionally imported from structured records;
  • or also inferred from observed behavioral evidence.
2. Whether “recognition” means:
  • user confirmation;
  • system-supported interpretation;
  • or both as separate fields.
3. Whether objective environmental data may include:
  • only externally sourced facts;
  • historically reconstructed conditions;
  • modeled estimates;
  • or all three with separate evidence grades.
4. Whether portal definitions are:
  • fixed and versioned;
  • configurable by administrators;
  • or extensible by modality.
5. Whether confidence is:
  • a deterministic score;
  • a rule-based classification;
  • or a hybrid score plus explanation.

Dependencies

None.

Acceptance criteria

• Every major term has one canonical definition.
• Objective environmental presence, subjective exposure, portal relevance, developmental effect, and convergence confidence are represented as separate concepts.
• Recognition status is not conflated with objective support or subjective exposure.
• Future forecast data is explicitly distinguishable from historical lived evidence.
• All unresolved design questions have an owner and decision deadline.
• No implementation team begins modality or convergence work before the domain decisions are approved.

